<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- Picker: SELECT and DESCRIBE inside declared databases, the two
       front ends over them, and nothing else.

       The R1.2 discipline of Fettler.csproj applies here whole, restated
       rather than referenced because this file has to stand alone:

       NO ProjectReference, EVER - not to Fettler, not to burler, not to
       anything. Picker shares this repository, its release and its
       version number with what else lives here, and shares nothing else.
       Where behaviour must match across programs - the tier-one screening
       patterns, the burler wire - each side carries its own copy, proven
       equivalent by test rather than by a shared assembly.

       A PackageReference only from the allowlist below, each entry
       meeting ALL of: a permissive licence (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD) and
       never copyleft; managed code that publishes clean as a
       self-contained single file per OS; a minimal transitive closure;
       and actively maintained. Picker.Tests asserts the resulting
       reference set from outside, because a constraint that is not
       tested lasts until somebody is in a hurry.

       The allowlist, in full:

         Microsoft.SqlServer.TransactSql.ScriptDom - MIT, pure managed,
         Microsoft's own T-SQL parser. It is here because the query gate
         PARSES rather than pattern-matches: one statement, a SELECT,
         every object and column reference resolved inside the grant,
         and any construct the walker does not recognise refused rather
         than passed unchecked.

         Microsoft.Data.SqlClient - MIT, the connection to the server.
         It carries a native SNI asset on Windows BY DEFAULT, and this
         project pins the MANAGED network path instead (D2 of the
         requirements): macOS and Linux only have the managed
         implementation, macOS is a required target, so pick ships one
         network stack on three OSes and the publish stays single-file
         with nothing extracted beside it. The pin is the
         UseManagedNetworkingOnWindows switch, set both here as a
         runtime host option and in pick's Main before any connection
         opens. If a real connection scenario ever fails on the managed
         path, re-admitting the native asset on Windows alone is the
         recorded fallback - and it is an allowlist decision, made in
         this comment, not a quiet csproj edit.

       Inside this one assembly the layering is Fettler's R3.1, kept by
       namespace and asserted by test: Picker.Core's public surface may
       not take or return a System.Text.Json type, name System.Console,
       or know an exit code. A core that speaks a protocol has stopped
       being one; the two front ends live beside it in Picker.Cli and
       Picker.Mcp and are ordinary classes, so the tests drive both
       without a process. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Picker</RootNamespace>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.SqlServer.TransactSql.ScriptDom" Version="180.59.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="7.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="Switch.Microsoft.Data.SqlClient.UseManagedNetworkingOnWindows"
                                    Value="true" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
